<rss version="2.0" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/"><channel><title>Field Notes &amp; Guides</title><link>https://demo.example.com/posts</link><description>Practical engineering notes</description><language>en</language><item><title>Designing useful product search</title><link>https://demo.example.com/posts/catalog-search</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://demo.example.com/posts/catalog-search</guid><pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 09:00:00 GMT</pubDate><description>A small catalog needs clear ranking, stable identifiers and useful empty states.</description><content:encoded>&lt;h1&gt;Designing useful product search&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A product catalog often fits comfortably in memory. For a shop with a few thousand products, an embedded full-text index can provide useful results without an external search cluster. The important questions are about relevance and operations: what gets indexed, when it changes, and how a user understands the result.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Index the words customers use&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Product titles deserve more weight than long descriptions. A search for &lt;strong&gt;desk lamp&lt;/strong&gt; should favor a lamp whose title contains both words over a desk whose care instructions mention a lamp. Include category names and common terminology in descriptions, but avoid filling every record with unrelated keywords.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-ts&quot;&gt;const fields = [&amp;quot;title&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;description&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;category&amp;quot;];
const boost = { title: 3, category: 2 };
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prefix matching helps while a customer is typing. Fuzzy matching can rescue a misspelling, but an aggressive edit distance makes short queries noisy. Set limits for query length and result count, and measure relevance with a small collection of real queries before changing defaults.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Keep identifiers stable&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A SKU should identify the same item across indexing, checkout and stock updates. Display names can change; identifiers should not. Store only the fields required by the result card in the index, then fetch authoritative pricing and availability before accepting an order.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Search use&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Checkout use&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Title&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ranked text and result label&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Human-readable description&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Category&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Filter and ranking boost&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tax and fulfillment rules&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Price&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Display snapshot&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Revalidate before purchase&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stock&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Availability hint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reserve atomically&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An in-memory index is a snapshot. Rebuild it after a catalog update or replace it atomically with a new version. Mutating a live index incrementally is possible, but failed updates need a recovery path and concurrent requests need a clear consistency model.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Treat empty results as a product decision&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Return a successful empty result for an ordinary query with no matches. Reserve client errors for malformed parameters. Show the original query, offer a spelling suggestion when confidence is high, and keep category navigation available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Preserve the query when the customer changes a filter.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Show a bounded number of results with an explicit total.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use a stable tie-breaker when relevance scores are equal.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep raw relevance scores internal unless an API consumer needs them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Know when to move beyond an embedded index&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A larger catalog, multilingual analysis, frequent writes or complex faceting may justify a separate service. Track index size and startup behavior before that move. A local index still needs an update strategy and monitoring; fewer moving parts do not remove the need for operational care.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The demonstration catalog uses generated, repeatable merchandising data. It exercises indexing and query handling, while relevance decisions for a real store should be evaluated against that store&amp;#39;s actual products and customer searches.&lt;/p&gt;
